    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$1.60 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e for a dish that reads like a special occasion. The beans are the cheapest thing in the pot and they are also the reason it works.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBeans are the soul of a cassoulet, and beans are cheap.\u003c/strong\u003e Canned cannellini save you the overnight soak for very little extra money, and they hold their shape through a long bake.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eSausage and bacon are seasonings here.\u003c/strong\u003e Between them they flavor the entire pot, so you do not need much of either. Buy the larger packs and freeze the balance in portions.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned diced tomatoes on case sale.\u003c/strong\u003e This bake goes through a real stack of them, so buy when the shelf tag drops and store them. They last for years.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCrack the crust and stir it back in, twice.\u003c/strong\u003e That is the classic move and it costs nothing. Every time you break the top and push it under, the whole dish gets creamier."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice separately per the package and set it aside. It goes under the cassoulet in the containers.",
                    "Brown the sliced hot Italian sausage and the chopped bacon in the olive oil in a heavy oven-safe pot, then set the meat aside and leave the fat.",
                    "Cook the diced onion and sliced carrots in that fat until soft, then stir in the minced garlic, Italian seasoning, dried thyme and red pepper flakes.",
                    "Add the diced tomatoes with their juice, the drained cannellini beans, the browned sausage and bacon, and the shredded turkey breast. Season with salt and black pepper.",
                    "Bring it to a simmer on the stove, cover, and bake at 325F for about 90 minutes.",
                    "Uncover for the last 30 minutes so a crust forms on top, breaking that crust and stirring it under once or twice as it bakes."
                ],
